WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.067
Day 12 of the 31 days of QA + AI series.

00:00:04.617 --> 00:00:09.356
Failure triage with LLMs: from 40 red tests to 3 root causes.

00:00:09.906 --> 00:00:14.560
A triage step that groups a run's failures by probable cause, with evidence, and

00:00:14.560 --> 00:00:16.829
says "unknown" when there isn't enough.

00:00:18.129 --> 00:00:22.137
A pipeline with forty red tests almost never has forty problems.

00:00:22.437 --> 00:00:27.930
It has two or three: a service that's down, an interface change and, with luck, a

00:00:27.930 --> 00:00:28.744
real defect.

00:00:29.044 --> 00:00:33.372
The job of whoever does triage is to discover that grouping, and it's slow,

00:00:33.372 --> 00:00:38.048
repetitive work: opening failure after failure, reading the error and recognizing

00:00:38.048 --> 00:00:40.703
you've already seen it ten times that morning.

00:00:42.003 --> 00:00:45.802
Grouping similar text is something an LLM does well and fast.

00:00:46.102 --> 00:00:48.717
What it doesn't do well is admit it doesn't know.

00:00:49.017 --> 00:00:53.724
This guide builds an assisted triage that uses the former and guards against the

00:00:53.724 --> 00:00:58.667
latter: small, structured input, output with textual evidence, a mandatory "unknown"

00:00:58.667 --> 00:01:02.138
category and a person who confirms before closing anything.

00:01:03.438 --> 00:01:05.136
To follow this guide you need:

00:01:05.686 --> 00:01:08.255
A Playwright suite with some failures.

00:01:08.805 --> 00:01:10.990
Traces enabled on retries.

00:01:12.290 --> 00:01:13.570
This is the setup.

00:01:13.870 --> 00:01:18.087
Pause the video if you want to copy it: the full version is in the written guide.

00:01:19.387 --> 00:01:21.653
And this is the next setup file.

00:01:22.953 --> 00:01:25.220
And this is the next setup file.

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:27.556
Step 1.

00:01:27.856 --> 00:01:29.182
Structured results.

00:01:29.732 --> 00:01:34.637
Emit results in a structured format with the JSON reporter and keep traces from

00:01:34.637 --> 00:01:35.133
retries.

00:01:35.683 --> 00:01:40.240
The starting point is having your runner emit results in a format a machine can

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:42.605
read, not just an HTML report for people.

00:01:42.905 --> 00:01:48.552
Playwright's JSON reporter includes each test's title, file, status, error and

00:01:48.552 --> 00:01:49.131
retries.

00:01:49.431 --> 00:01:54.038
Also enable traces on first retry: they are the evidence you'll need to verify

00:01:54.038 --> 00:01:55.691
whatever the model proposes.

00:01:55.991 --> 00:02:00.398
Without structured data you end up pasting whole logs into a chat, which is

00:02:00.398 --> 00:02:02.983
expensive, slow and mixes noise with signal.

00:02:04.283 --> 00:02:05.482
Step 2.

00:02:05.782 --> 00:02:07.642
Extract only what's needed.

00:02:08.192 --> 00:02:13.384
Extract failures only: title, file and the first 400 characters of the error.

00:02:13.684 --> 00:02:15.603
Fewer tokens, better grouping.

00:02:16.153 --> 00:02:21.133
A short script walks the JSON and keeps the failed tests: title, file and the first

00:02:21.133 --> 00:02:23.353
four hundred characters of the error.

00:02:23.653 --> 00:02:25.572
That trimming has two effects.

00:02:25.872 --> 00:02:30.425
It lowers cost, because a full log can hold thousands of irrelevant lines.

00:02:30.725 --> 00:02:35.858
And it improves the result, because the first part of the message usually holds what

00:02:35.858 --> 00:02:40.318
tells one cause from another: the error type, the locator, the HTTP code.

00:02:40.618 --> 00:02:45.404
With full stack traces, the model tends to group by superficial stack matches.

00:02:46.704 --> 00:02:47.786
Step 3.

00:02:48.086 --> 00:02:49.389
The triage prompt.

00:02:49.939 --> 00:02:51.590
Run the triage prompt.

00:02:51.890 --> 00:02:56.131
Require category, tests in the group and the exact evidence line.

00:02:56.681 --> 00:03:01.353
Ask for three things per group: a category from a closed list, the tests in it and

00:03:01.353 --> 00:03:03.916
the exact error line justifying the grouping.

00:03:04.216 --> 00:03:09.745
The closed list matters: environment, data, interface change, probable defect and

00:03:09.745 --> 00:03:10.291
unknown.

00:03:10.591 --> 00:03:15.075
The evidence line must be a literal quote from the input, not an explanation.

00:03:15.375 --> 00:03:19.631
That way you can check in seconds whether the group makes sense, and a group whose

00:03:19.631 --> 00:03:22.901
evidence doesn't appear in the data gets discarded immediately.

00:03:24.201 --> 00:03:25.214
Step 4.

00:03:25.514 --> 00:03:27.583
Verify the largest group.

00:03:28.133 --> 00:03:33.209
Manually verify the largest group by opening a trace with npx playwright show-trace.

00:03:33.759 --> 00:03:38.580
Before acting on the result, open the trace of one test from the largest group.

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:43.352
Look at the timeline, the network calls and the screenshot at the moment of failure.

00:03:43.652 --> 00:03:47.990
If the proposed cause matches what you see, the group is confirmed and you've

00:03:47.990 --> 00:03:50.412
probably resolved most of the red pipeline.

00:03:50.712 --> 00:03:54.151
If it doesn't match, treat the whole result with more caution.

00:03:54.451 --> 00:03:59.318
That check takes a few minutes and is what turns a suggestion into a diagnosis.

00:04:00.618 --> 00:04:01.747
Step 5.

00:04:02.047 --> 00:04:03.594
Save every triage.

00:04:04.144 --> 00:04:06.747
Save each triage with your final verdict.

00:04:07.047 --> 00:04:10.324
In a few weeks you have a dataset to evaluate the prompt.

00:04:10.874 --> 00:04:15.753
Save the input, the model's output and your final verdict in one file per run.

00:04:16.053 --> 00:04:20.572
It looks like bureaucracy and it's the most valuable part of the process.

00:04:20.872 --> 00:04:25.080
In a few weeks you have dozens of real cases labeled by someone who knows the

00:04:25.080 --> 00:04:29.617
system: exactly the dataset needed to evaluate the triage prompt with the day eight

00:04:29.617 --> 00:04:30.000
method.

00:04:30.300 --> 00:04:35.030
You'll also see patterns a single day doesn't show, such as a test that always lands

00:04:35.030 --> 00:04:36.607
in the environment category.

00:04:37.907 --> 00:04:39.093
Step 6.

00:04:39.393 --> 00:04:40.754
Closure is human.

00:04:41.304 --> 00:04:46.776
Don't automate closure: the model proposes, a person confirms before quarantining or

00:04:46.776 --> 00:04:47.623
filing a bug.

00:04:48.173 --> 00:04:53.503
The model proposes groups and causes; a person confirms before quarantining a test,

00:04:53.503 --> 00:04:55.943
filing a bug or retrying the pipeline.

00:04:56.243 --> 00:04:58.149
The reason is the cost of error.

00:04:58.449 --> 00:05:02.601
Wrongly quarantining a test that was catching a real defect lets that defect

00:05:02.601 --> 00:05:03.038
through.

00:05:03.338 --> 00:05:06.998
Filing twenty duplicate bugs costs QA its credibility.

00:05:07.298 --> 00:05:11.761
Automating the proposal saves the tedious part; automating the decision moves the

00:05:11.761 --> 00:05:14.185
risk to a system that doesn't answer for it.

00:05:15.485 --> 00:05:16.591
An example.

00:05:17.141 --> 00:05:20.649
On a Monday the nightly pipeline shows forty-two failures.

00:05:20.949 --> 00:05:25.735
The script extracts the forty-two trimmed errors and the prompt returns four groups.

00:05:26.035 --> 00:05:30.744
Thirty-one share the line of a 503 error from the authentication service:

00:05:30.744 --> 00:05:31.518
environment.

00:05:31.818 --> 00:05:36.523
Seven have a timeout waiting for a button whose name changed: interface change.

00:05:36.823 --> 00:05:41.388
Three show a cart total different from the expected one: probable defect.

00:05:41.688 --> 00:05:43.339
One stays as unknown.

00:05:43.639 --> 00:05:48.413
The QA opens a trace from the first group and confirms the 503.

00:05:48.713 --> 00:05:53.608
She retries the pipeline once the service is back, updates one locator and files a

00:05:53.608 --> 00:05:55.996
single bug with three tests as evidence.

00:05:56.296 --> 00:05:59.456
The unknown case turns out to be expired test data.

00:06:00.756 --> 00:06:02.419
The most common mistakes.

00:06:02.969 --> 00:06:05.862
Pasting the full log instead of the trimmed errors.

00:06:06.413 --> 00:06:10.154
Not offering the unknown category and getting invented causes.

00:06:10.704 --> 00:06:14.340
Accepting the model's explanation without opening any trace.

00:06:14.890 --> 00:06:18.329
Automating quarantine or bug filing without confirmation.

00:06:19.629 --> 00:06:21.873
To know it is done, check this.

00:06:22.423 --> 00:06:25.885
The runner emits JSON and keeps traces on retries.

00:06:26.435 --> 00:06:30.699
The script extracts title, file and trimmed error for each failure.

00:06:31.249 --> 00:06:33.968
Each group comes with a literal evidence line.

00:06:34.518 --> 00:06:36.924
The triage is saved with your final verdict.

00:06:38.224 --> 00:06:40.119
And where AI fell short.

00:06:40.669 --> 00:06:44.793
If you don't give it the "unknown" exit, it fabricates a plausible cause.

00:06:45.093 --> 00:06:47.580
An invented cause is worse than none.

00:06:48.880 --> 00:06:50.450
That was day 12.

00:06:50.750 --> 00:06:55.725
The written guide, at gastongenaud.com, has the code to copy and the frequently

00:06:55.725 --> 00:06:56.732
asked questions.

00:06:57.282 --> 00:07:03.334
Continue with day 13: TDD with AI (1): red, green, refactor with an agent.
